; ── Nintendo RF Modulator (NUS-003) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; A rigid black block whose MULTI OUT plug goes straight into an N64's AV MULTI OUT
; (no cord), an RCA "RF OUT" jack on its far end for the RF switch's console lead,
; and a CH3-CH4 slide beside that jack. RfModulator explains the lead it is: ONE
; plug, PlugA0, which IS the block, no PlugB0 and no VerletRope.
;
; ── The frame ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; PlugA0's frame is the N64 AV lead's console plug's (n64_av_cable.tscn): the origin
; on the MATING plane (the face that stops on the socket's bezel, 2 mm proud of the
; panel, n64_av_port.tscn), the connector along +Z into the console, the body
; behind it along -Z. A seated plug stands at zone * R_x(180) (its SnapGrabPoint),
; so on the N64's marker (+Y up, +Z out of the back) PlugA0's -Y is UP: the block is
; laid out upside down in this frame, and stands upright on the console.
;
; RetroXR-models' nintendo/n64/rf-modulator GLB (glTF: meters, +Y up, base at y 0,
; the nose pointing -Z, its tip at (0, 0.0105, -0.03695)) goes on PlugA0 by ONE
; fixed transform. Its mating plane is the nose's ROOT, 12.8 mm (the nose's length)
; back from the tip, at glTF (0, 0.0105, -0.02415), the collar's face:
;
;   p_PlugA0 = R_x(180) * (p_glTF - (0, 0.0105, -0.02415))
;   i.e. Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0.0105, -0.02415)   (.tscn rows)
;        x -> x, y -> 0.0105 - y, z -> -0.02415 - z
;
; Everything below is that map applied to the model's numbers:
;   block   37.5 x 22.5 x 41.3, glTF y 0..0.0225, z -0.02065..0.02065
;           -> PlugA0 y -0.012..0.0105, z -0.0448..-0.0035
;   collar  24 x 11, 3.5 out of the AV IN end -> z -0.0035..0
;   nose    19.4 x 6.8 x 12.8 -> z 0..0.0128 (inside the console when seated)
;   foot    6.6 x 7.5, 5 below the base, under the AV IN end's RF OUT-side corner
;   RfOut   glTF mouth (-0.0100, 0.0054, 0.02985), mouth facing +Z
;           -> (-0.0100, 0.0051, -0.0540), basis R_x(180) (mouth facing PlugA0 -Z)
;   knob    glTF ChannelPivot (0.0035, 0.0082, 0.02065), CH3, slides +X 3 mm
;           -> ChannelKnob at (0.0035, 0.0023, -0.0448); the VRSlider ChannelSwitch
;              at mid-travel (0.0050, 0.0023, -0.0448), axis +X (x maps to x)
;
; ── For a mod that dresses it ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Hide PlugA0/Shell (every stand-in mesh but the knob), add the GLB under PlugA0 at
; the transform above, set PlugA0/RfOut's show_jack false (the model moulds its
; jack), and hand the model's knob to RfModulator.adopt_channel_knob (it hides
; ChannelKnob). The stand-in is named Shell on purpose: a Multi Out plug that
; already wears a Shell is one a lead mod (xenu.n64av dresses "n64_av_plug") leaves
; alone, and this is not that lead's plug.
[node name="N64RfModulator" type="Node3D"]
script = ExtResource("1_modscript")
cord_colors = PackedColorArray(0.1, 0.1, 0.105, 1)

; Turned R_x(180) so the block stands upright as spawned (see the frame above).
[node name="PlugA0" type="RigidBody3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0)
collision_layer = 4
collision_mask = 196613
physics_material_override = SubResource("PhysMat_block")
mass = 0.06
linear_damp = 5.0
angular_damp = 8.0
freeze_mode = 1
script = ExtResource("2_plugscript")
connector = "n64_av_plug"

